WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.466
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:17.736
Unit twenty-three: Project Management. Today you will describe actions in progress at a future time with the future continuous.

4
00:00:18.036 --> 00:00:20.642
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.158
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:26.844
Project.

7
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:31.800
Project means a planned piece of work with a specific goal and deadline.

8
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:35.206
The bridge project is three months behind schedule.

9
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:38.504
Your turn. Say: Project.

10
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.101
Project.

11
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:43.747
Scope.

12
00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.212
Scope means the agreed boundaries of what a project will deliver.

13
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:52.802
Adding new features without approval is called scope creep.

14
00:00:53.417 --> 00:00:55.663
Your turn. Say: Scope.

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:00:59.397
Scope.

16
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:00.977
Timeline.

17
00:01:01.983 --> 00:01:05.121
Timeline means a schedule showing when tasks will happen.

18
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:08.273
The timeline allows two weeks for testing.

19
00:01:08.883 --> 00:01:12.058
Your turn. Say: Timeline.

20
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:15.773
Timeline.

21
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:17.738
Milestone.

22
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.479
Milestone means a significant point or achievement in a project.

23
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.814
Finishing the foundations was a major milestone.

24
00:01:26.417 --> 00:01:29.023
Your turn. Say: Milestone.

25
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:32.833
Milestone.

26
00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:34.875
Resources.

27
00:01:35.883 --> 00:01:39.984
Resources means the people, money and materials available for a project.

28
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:43.221
We lack the resources to finish by June.

29
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:46.713
Your turn. Say: Resources.

30
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:50.777
Resources.

31
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:52.274
Risk.

32
00:01:53.283 --> 00:01:56.316
Risk means a possible event that could harm the project.

33
00:01:56.917 --> 00:01:59.473
Heavy rain is the biggest risk during construction.

34
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:02.724
Your turn. Say: Risk.

35
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:06.568
Risk.

36
00:02:07.317 --> 00:02:08.353
Deliverable.

37
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:13.161
Deliverable means a specific output a project must produce.

38
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.539
The first deliverable is a working prototype.

39
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.895
Your turn. Say: Deliverable.

40
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:23.851
Deliverable.

41
00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:25.386
Agile.

42
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:30.229
Agile means a flexible method that delivers work in short cycles.

43
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:33.722
Agile teams review progress every two weeks.

44
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:36.632
Your turn. Say: Agile.

45
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:40.328
Agile.

46
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.854
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:50.784
Visal, the school's new learning app is due in December. Where will the team be next month?

48
00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:59.781
By next month, the team will be testing the app with two hundred students, so we'll be collecting feedback every day.

49
00:03:00.883 --> 00:03:03.521
And what if testing reveals serious problems?

50
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:12.935
That's why we built in a buffer. While the developers are fixing bugs, the designers will be preparing the training videos.

51
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:16.584
Isn't the client asking for extra features?

52
00:03:17.683 --> 00:03:26.851
Constantly. But we'll be protecting the scope. New requests go into phase two; otherwise the deadline will slip.

53
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.489
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:33.290
Grammar focus: Future continuous.

55
00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:34.800
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:35.517 --> 00:03:39.084
By next month, the team will be delivering the final product.

57
00:03:39.983 --> 00:03:43.550
This time next week, we will be testing the app with students.

58
00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.191
While the developers fix bugs, the designers will be preparing videos.

59
00:03:49.133 --> 00:04:00.400
Use the future continuous for actions in progress at a future moment (this time tomorrow, we'll be travelling) and for planned events (we'll be meeting the client on Friday). Do not use it with state verbs like know or own.

60
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.882
Be careful! Don't say: Tomorrow we will delivering it.

61
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:09.077
Say: Tomorrow we will be delivering it.

62
00:04:09.377 --> 00:04:11.783
Future continuous: will be + -ing.

63
00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:15.196
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.396
Why Projects Fail.

65
00:04:17.696 --> 00:04:23.469
Project management aims to deliver a defined scope on time, within budget and to the required quality.

66
00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:32.376
Studies suggest that most failures result not from technical difficulties but from unclear goals, poor communication and uncontrolled scope creep.

67
00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:41.171
Traditional 'waterfall' planning sets the entire timeline in advance, whereas agile methods deliver small, usable increments every few weeks.

68
00:04:42.167 --> 00:04:48.253
A good project manager constantly asks what the team will be doing at each milestone and which risks could derail it.

69
00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:55.690
Ultimately, successful projects depend as much on managing people and expectations as on managing tasks.

70
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.639
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.483 --> 00:05:04.284
This time tomorrow, we blank the client.

72
00:05:04.584 --> 00:05:08.295
A, will meet. B, will be meeting. C, are meet.

73
00:05:11.833 --> 00:05:14.100
The answer is B: will be meeting.

74
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.561
The agreed boundaries of what a project will deliver is its blank.

75
00:05:18.861 --> 00:05:22.097
A, scope. B, timeline. C, risk.

76
00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:27.505
The answer is A: scope.

77
00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.147
A significant point or achievement in a project is a blank.

78
00:05:32.447 --> 00:05:36.658
A, resource. B, deliverable. C, milestone.

79
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.281
The answer is C: milestone.

80
00:05:43.417 --> 00:05:45.184
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:45.484 --> 00:05:52.980
A, At 10 a.m. we will be presenting the plan.. B, At 10 a.m. we will presenting the plan.. C, At 10 a.m. we be presenting the plan..

82
00:05:56.533 --> 00:06:00.193
The answer is A: At 10 a.m. we will be presenting the plan..

83
00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:05.045
According to the reading, most project failures result from…

84
00:06:05.345 --> 00:06:11.192
A, technical difficulties. B, unclear goals and poor communication. C, too much testing.

85
00:06:14.733 --> 00:06:18.567
The answer is B: unclear goals and poor communication.

86
00:06:19.767 --> 00:06:22.965
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.817 --> 00:06:25.816
What causes projects to fail?

88
00:06:42.617 --> 00:06:44.256
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:44.556 --> 00:07:24.136
Projects usually fail because of unclear goals, unrealistic deadlines and poor communication rather than purely technical problems. When the scope keeps expanding without extra time or money, the team becomes overloaded and quality falls. For example, a construction project may collapse into delays if the client keeps changing the design. Another cause is ignoring risks such as the rainy season. In my view, a project succeeds when everyone knows exactly what they will be doing, why it matters and when it is due.

90
00:07:24.436 --> 00:07:26.122
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:27.017 --> 00:07:31.132
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future continuous.

92
00:07:31.432 --> 00:07:36.058
Today's value is planning ahead. Make a plan and take small steps every day.

93
00:07:36.358 --> 00:07:39.115
See you in Unit twenty-four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.415 --> 00:07:42.578
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

